course: dictionary
lesson: 12446
title: Decreed: an official order
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When a ruler or a government decreed something, they gave an official order that everyone had to follow. «The mayor decreed that the park would close at sunset.» The words official order and close at sunset show you that this is a rule, not a wish.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Words like king, government, council, law or emperor often come before decreed. «The king decreed that every village must build a well.» A king does not ask nicely; he orders, so decreed means ordered officially.
explain: In old stories, decreed can also mean something fixed that must happen. «It was decreed that the dragon would sleep for a hundred years.» Nobody could change it, so it was certain. Ask yourself: is someone giving an order, or is it fate deciding?
fact: Decree is the verb, and decreed is its past tense, so decreed is what happened in the past.
fact: A decree (the noun) is an official order from a ruler or government, like a new law.
fact: «Decreed» means officially ordered, or, in old stories, fixed and certain to happen.
fact: Check your meaning by swapping it in: «The king officially ordered that every village must build a well» still makes sense.
fact: Decree is a formal word, so you will meet it most in history, law, Bible stories and fairy tales.
exercise: «The mayor decreed that the park would close at sunset.» What does decreed mean? | Argued about | *Officially ordered | Painted | Forgotten
exercise: Find and copy the noun that means an official order: «The king's new decree was read aloud in the square.» | aloud | *decree | read | square
exercise: «It was decreed that the dragon would sleep for a hundred years.» What does decreed suggest in this old story? | A kind wish that might not come true | A loud noise | A lost treasure | *Something fixed that had to happen
test: «The emperor decreed that everyone must be counted.» Which word tells you the emperor gave an official order? | emperor | *decreed | counted | everyone
test: Ella says decreed means wished. Why is she wrong? | *A decree is an official order, not a wish | Wishes and orders mean exactly the same | It is a kind of food | Decreed means painted softly
exam: «The law decreed that no one could fish in the lake.» Which word shows the law was an official order? | fish | *decreed | lake | law
